<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portobelo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Porto Bello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portobelo is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, founded in 1597 to replace Nombre de Dios as the Atlantic end of the treasure route across the isthmus. Its fairs, held when the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> arrived, exchanged Peruvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for European goods, and its forts were sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a> in 1668 and taken by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-vernon">Edward Vernon</a> in 1739. With nearby San Lorenzo, its fortifications are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portobelo.jpg" alt="Portobelo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tedder · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portobelo_Ruins_and_bay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Portobelo lies on the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-panama">Isthmus of Panama</a>, in Colón Province, 32 km northeast of the modern port of Colón at the Atlantic entrance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a>. It was chosen for its deep natural harbour. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> named it Puerto Bello, meaning beautiful port, in 1502. After the English privateer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> died of dysentery in 1596, he was buried at sea in a lead coffin off Portobelo, and Drake Island at the mouth of the harbour commemorates him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish explorer Francisco Velarde y Mercado founded Portobelo in 1597, and it quickly replaced Nombre de Dios as the major local port for Peruvian silver. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> it was one of the two Atlantic ports on the route of the Spanish treasure fleet, and the Spanish built fortifications to protect it. English privateers led by William Parker captured it in 1601. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a> did so in 1668 with 450 privateers and plundered it for 14 days; the English pirate John Coxon took it again in 1680.</p><p>In 1726 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> under Admiral Francis Hosier blockaded the port to stop the treasure fleet returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; Hosier and many of his sailors died of tropical disease at Bastimentos. On 21 November 1739 a British squadron under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-vernon">Edward Vernon</a> captured it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a>. More medals were struck for Vernon than for any other 18th-century British figure, and places across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> were named Portobello, among them Portobello Road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and the Portobello Barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><p>The Spanish recovered Portobelo in 1741 after their victory at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a>. British efforts to gain a foothold on the Spanish Main and disrupt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade failed. Afterwards the Spanish used small fleets trading at many ports, which made them less open to attack, and ships began to sail around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-horn">Cape Horn</a> to trade directly on the western coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Portobelo is the seat of Portobelo District, and its economy received a boost in the late 19th century during the construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a>. In 1980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the Fortifications on the Caribbean Side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>: Portobelo-San Lorenzo, the ruins of the Spanish colonial fortifications and nearby Fort San Lorenzo, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. In July 2012 the World Heritage Committee placed the site on the List of World Heritage in Danger, citing environmental factors, lack of maintenance and uncontrolled urban development.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portobelo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Panama's role was transit: silver from Potosí was shipped to Panama City, carried across the isthmus by mule and river, and traded at the great fairs of Nombre de Dios and later <b>Portobelo</b> for European goods.</blockquote><a href="/state/real-audiencia-of-panama">Real Audiencia of Panama</a></li></ul></section>
